1
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:40,838
What Happens In Antarctica... Stays in Antarctica

2
00:05:09,677 --> 00:05:15,677
Farce of the Penguins is a comedy, romance,
documentary film directed by Bob Saget.

3
00:07:44,516 --> 00:07:50,516
In this spoof of "March of the Penguins," nature footage of penguins
near the South Pole gets a soundtrack of human voices.

4
00:10:19,354 --> 00:10:25,354
Carl and Jimmy, best friends, walk 70 miles to the mating
grounds where the female penguins wait.

5
00:12:54,193 --> 00:13:00,193
The huddled masses of females - especially Melissa and Vicki - talk
about males, mating, and what might happen this year.

6
00:15:29,032 --> 00:15:35,032
Carl, Jimmy, and the other males make the long trek
talking about food, fornication and flatulence.

7
00:18:03,870 --> 00:18:09,870
Until this year, Carl's sex life has been dismal, but he falls hard for Melissa.

8
00:20:38,709 --> 00:20:44,709
She seems to like him.

9
00:23:13,548 --> 00:23:19,548
A crisis develops when Jimmy comes upon something soft in the dark.

10
00:25:48,387 --> 00:25:54,387
Can friends forgive?

11
00:28:23,225 --> 00:28:29,225
Does parenthood await Carl and Melissa?

12
00:30:58,064 --> 00:31:04,064
Narrador (voz), played by Samuel L. Jackson.

13
00:33:32,903 --> 00:33:38,903
Hottie Penguin (voz), played by Alyson Hannigan.

14
00:36:07,741 --> 00:36:13,741
Carl (voz), played by Bob Saget.

15
00:38:42,580 --> 00:38:48,580
I Need a Z-Pack Penguin (voz), played by Brie Larson.

16
00:41:17,419 --> 00:41:23,419
Juan Sanchez (voz), played by Carlos Mencia.

17
00:43:52,258 --> 00:43:58,258
There's No Snow Penguin (voz), played by Dave Coulier.

18
00:46:27,096 --> 00:46:33,096
March of the Penguins (French: La Marche de l'empereur; French pronunciation: [lamaʁʃ
dəlɑ̃ˈpʁœʁ]) is a 2005 French nature documentary film directed and written by Luc Jacquet.

19
00:49:01,935 --> 00:49:07,935
The documentary depicts the yearly journey of the
emperor penguins of Antarctica.

20
00:51:36,774 --> 00:51:42,774
In autumn, all the penguins of breeding age (five years old and over) leave the ocean, which
is their normal habitat, to walk inland to their ancestral breeding grounds.

21
00:54:11,612 --> 00:54:17,612
There, the penguins participate in a courtship that, if
successful, results in the hatching of a chick.

22
00:56:46,451 --> 00:56:52,451
For the chick to survive, both parents must make multiple arduous journeys
between the ocean and the breeding grounds over the ensuing months.

23
00:59:21,290 --> 00:59:27,290
It took one year for the two isolated cinematographers Laurent Chalet and Jérôme Maison to shoot the
documentary, which was shot around the French scientific base of Dumont d'Urville in Adélie Land.

24
01:01:56,129 --> 01:02:02,129
March of the Penguins was released in France on 26 January
2005 by Buena Vista International France.

25
01:04:30,967 --> 01:04:36,967
The documentary won the 2006 Academy Awards for Best Documentary Feature.

26
01:07:05,806 --> 01:07:11,806
On 1 June 2010, a home video release in France included
the film in the Disneynature collection.

27
01:09:40,645 --> 01:09:46,645
It is named as one of the most successful
documentaries in North America and France.

28
01:12:15,483 --> 01:12:21,483
A direct sequel titled March of the Penguins 2: The Next Step
was released in France in 2017 by Disneynature.

29
01:14:50,322 --> 01:14:56,322
The film explores themes of penguin, mockumentary,
balzen, antarctica, found footage.

30
01:17:25,161 --> 01:17:31,161
The tone is lighthearted and dark and emotional.
